arXiv:2507.14850v1 [cs.LG] 20 Jul 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">H. M. Sabbir Ahmad<sup id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Ehsan Sabouni<sup id="id1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Alexander Wasilkoff<sup id="id1.1.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Param Budhraja<sup id="id1.1.1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>,</span></span>
<span id="id1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Zijian Guo<sup id="id1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup> Songyuan Zhang<sup id="id1.2.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Chuchu Fan<sup id="id1.2.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Christos Cassandras<sup id="id1.2.1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Wenchao Li<sup id="id1.2.1.5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup></span></span>
</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>Boston University, <sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>Massachusetts Institute of Technology
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{sabbir92,esabouni,awasilkoff,paramb,zjguo,cgc,wenchao}@bu.edu
<br class="ltx_break">{szhang21,chuchu}@mit.edu</span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We address the problem of safe policy learning in multi-agent safety-critical autonomous systems.
In such systems, it is necessary for each agent to meet the safety requirements at all times while also cooperating with other agents to accomplish the task. Toward this end, we propose a safe Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (HMARL) approach based on Control Barrier Functions (CBFs). Our proposed hierarchical approach decomposes the overall reinforcement learning problem into two levels –- learning joint cooperative behavior at the higher level and learning safe individual behavior at the lower or agent level conditioned on the high-level policy. Specifically, we propose a skill-based HMARL-CBF algorithm in which the higher-level problem involves learning a joint policy over the skills for all the agents and the lower-level problem involves
learning policies to execute the skills safely with CBFs. We validate our approach on challenging environment scenarios whereby a large number of agents have to safely navigate through conflicting road networks. Compared with existing state-of-the-art methods, our approach significantly improves the safety achieving near perfect (within <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math>) success/safety rate while also improving performance across all the environments.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety-critical multi-agent systems are ever-growing with applications spanning self-driving cars, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), swarm robotics, soft robotics, autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs), to name only a few. As these systems operate in complex and often unpredictable environments,
failure can result in catastrophic outcomes, including risks to human lives, environmental damage, or severe economic consequences.
It is, therefore, necessary to learn cooperative policies which can achieve the task while ensuring safety between agents and with respect to the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem is further exacerbated in partially observable settings. Learning a flat policy using existing Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> algorithms by adjoining the safety constraints to the reward function provides one possible approach; this, however, suffers from scalability and high sample complexity with the number of agents, and, most importantly, lacks safety guarantees. The problem of safety has been addressed in single and multi-agent RL using constrained Markov Decision Processes (CMDP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. However, this formulation considers the discounted sum of the constraints over a trajectory, thus enforcing safety in a statistical sense over trajectories as opposed to pointwise time constraints enforced over each entire trajectory, pertinent to safety-critical systems. A Hierarchical approach as proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> can alleviate sample complexity but existing methods do not tackle safety. Toward this end, we propose the following:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose HMARL - CBF - a novel CTDE Safety Guaranteed Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning approach based on CBFs for safety-critical cooperative multi-agent partially observable systems. These systems have safety constraints that have to be satisfied at all times.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach adopts
a hierarchical structure based on skills/options, where the high-level policy leverages skills to optimize the cooperative behavior among agents, while the low-level policy is dedicated to learning and executing safe skills.
It is worth emphasizing that this hierarchical design guarantees safety during both the training phase and real-world deployment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our proposed approach on challenging conflicting environment scenarios where a large number of agents must each travel safely from its origin to its destination without colliding with other agents. Simulation results demonstrate superior performance and safety compliance achieving near perfect (<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 95\%</annotation></semantics></math>) success/safety rate compared to existing benchmark methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Agent RL.</span> MARL provides a solution for cooperative multi-agent games, commonly following the Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution (CTDE) scheme to improve coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Value decomposition methods (e.g., VDN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, QMIX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, QTRAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>) improve credit assignment by factorizing the joint value function, while policy gradient methods (e.g., MADDPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, COMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>) use centralized critics to stabilize learning. However, safety remains unaddressed, as existing methods do not consider real-time constraints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical Multi-Agent RL (HMARL).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hierarchical RL structures decision-making hierarchically, accelerating learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Classical methods include HAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, MAXQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, options <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and feudal architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Deep HRL extends this with subgoal-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, option-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, and skill-based frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. HMARL introduces hierarchy in MARL for enhanced coordination and exploration. Notable methods include value decomposition (QTRAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, MAXQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, HAVEN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>), feudal structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and option-based hierarchies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Temporal abstraction is explored in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, while meta-policy learning appears in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Skill discovery in HRL has explored mutual information maximization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, trajectory clustering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and hierarchical skill identification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>,option learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, imitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and adaptive refinement via Skill-Critic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. However, these works define skills as fixed length or variable length sequences lack interpretable semantics and safety guarantees, limiting applicability to safety-critical systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safe RL</span> Safety in multi-agent systems is commonly addressed using the Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Approaches include primal methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and primal-dual methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. While CMDPs ensure safety over trajectories, they do not consider pointwise time constraints critical for safety-critical systems. Multi-agent scenarios further complicate this, as the number of constraints scales with agents. Primal-dual methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> also face issues with training instability, slow convergence, and hyperparameter sensitivity. Additionally, works addressing reach avoid problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> are primarily limited to single agent fully observable settings. Previous works on RL-CBFs have harnessed exact or approximate models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> to learn safe policies. The existing methods tackle safety for single-agent systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, and multi-agent systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> considers a multi-agent cooperative setting conditioned on a fixed coordination policy which can be suboptimal. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, solved constrained RL problem with CBF-based chance constraints using the augmented Lagrangian, which nullifies the merits of CBF filters. Additionally, existing works require a nominal policy which is learned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, however this, can be challenging for MAS. Finally, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> proposed a method for mulit-agent systems, but such methods suffer from high sample complexity and scalability due to the flat policy structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Semi-Markov Decision Process</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Semi-Markov Decision Process (SMDP) is a generalization of a MDP that allows actions to take variable amounts of time steps before transitioning to the next state. Building on that, similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, we model a Multi-Agent Semi-Markov Decision Process (MSMDP) as a tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{Z},P,R,\gamma,\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{Z},P,R,\gamma,\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> described as follows: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents, with each agent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> having an individual action set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The joint action space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of joint actions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}=\langle z^{1},\ldots,z^{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}=\langle z^{1},\ldots,z^{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the joint actions of the agents. The joint state space is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}S^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}S^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reward function is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Z}\times\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Z}\times\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the reward accrued executing action <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> steps, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor, and the multistep transition probability function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{N}\times\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Z}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{N}\times\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Z}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> gives the probability of transitioning from state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> time steps as a result of the joint action <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the joint actions may have varying termination times, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is influenced by the duration of executing an action from the state it is initiated until transitioning to the next state (also referred to as the decision epoch) and a termination scheme <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Three termination strategies for the temporally extended joint actions, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{any}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>any</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{any}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{all}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{all}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{continue}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>continue</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{continue}}</annotation></semantics></math>, are analyzed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. Synchronous schemes, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{any}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>any</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{any}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{all}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{all}}</annotation></semantics></math>, set decision epochs when either any or all actions within a joint action terminate, with all agents reselecting actions. In contrast, the asynchronous scheme <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{continue}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>continue</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{continue}}</annotation></semantics></math> allows agents to continue their actions independently until termination. Our decentralized approach adopts this asynchronous action update strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The equation corresponding to the state value function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and state-action value function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})</annotation></semantics></math> are defined below for a MSMDP:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\pi}(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\pi}(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\pi}}\left[Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{z},k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}V^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝝅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\pi}}\left[Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{z},k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}V^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q^{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}V^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{z}^{\prime}}\left[Q^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime},\bm{z}^{\prime})\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}V^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{k,\,\bm{s}^{\prime}}\left[R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)+\gamma^{k}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{z}^{\prime}}\left[Q^{\pi}(\bm{s}^{\prime},\bm{z}^{\prime})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> represents the time interval until agent(s) update their action, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the reward obtained by taking the joint action <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}</annotation></semantics></math> over the random time interval <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The learning objective is to find the joint policy <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> for all the agents that maximizes: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{t-1}k_{t^{\prime}}}R(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{z}_{t},k_{t})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.400em">t</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="1.400em">=</mo><mn mathsize="1.400em">0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{t-1}k_{t^{\prime}}}R(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{z}_{t},k_{t})]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Control Barrier Functions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following agent dynamics (with the agent index dropped for simplicity):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\dot{s}}=f(\bm{s})+g(\bm{s})\bm{a}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\dot{s}}=f(\bm{s})+g(\bm{s})\bm{a},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state of the agent, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are the primitive actions, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A continuous function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:[0,\beta)\rightarrow[0,\infty],\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:[0,\beta)\rightarrow[0,\infty],\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is said to belong to class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> if it is strictly increasing and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is forward invariant for system (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) if for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}(0)\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}(0)\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}(t)\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}(t)\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Control barrier function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a continuously differentiable function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:=\{\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:b(\bm{s})\geq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:=\{\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:b(\bm{s})\geq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is a candidate control barrier function (CBF) for the system (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) if there exists a class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}\left[L_{f}b(\bm{s})+L_{g}b(\bm{s})\bm{a}+\alpha(b(\bm{s}))\right]\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}\left[L_{f}b(\bm{s})+L_{g}b(\bm{s})\bm{a}+\alpha(b(\bm{s}))\right]\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f},L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f},L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Lie derivatives along <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. CBFs can be extended to higher order resulting in HOCBFs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a constraint <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\bm{s}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\bm{s}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> with the associated sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s as defined in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), any Lipschitz continuous controller <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, that satisfies (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Definition 3.6 (High Order CBF (HOCBF) ( ) ). ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t\geq t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t\geq t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> renders the sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (including the set corresponding to the actual safety constraint <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) forward invariant for control system (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Relative degree)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The relative degree of a (sufficiently many times) differentiable function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to system (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is the number of times it needs to be differentiated along its dynamics until the control <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly appears in the corresponding derivative.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a constraint <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\bm{s})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\bm{s})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> with relative degree <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,\ b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,\ b</annotation></semantics></math> : <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{0}(\bm{s}):=b(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{0}(\bm{s}):=b(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a sequence of functions <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R},i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R},i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> :</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}(\bm{s}):=\dot{\zeta}_{i-1}(\bm{s})+\alpha_{i}\left(\zeta_{i-1}(\bm{s})\right),\;i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ζ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}(\bm{s}):=\dot{\zeta}_{i-1}(\bm{s})+\alpha_{i}\left(\zeta_{i-1}(\bm{s})\right),\;i\in\{1,\ldots,m\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}(),\ i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}(),\ i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m-i)^{\text{th }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>th </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m-i)^{\text{th }}</annotation></semantics></math> order differentiable class <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function. We further define a sequence of sets <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i},\ i\in\{1,...,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i},\ i\in\{1,...,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) which take the following form,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}:=\{\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:\zeta_{i-1}(\bm{s})\geq 0\},\;i\in\{1,...,m\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}:=\{\bm{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:\zeta_{i-1}(\bm{s})\geq 0\},\;i\in\{1,...,m\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(High Order CBF (HOCBF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},...,C_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},...,C_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{1}(\bm{s}),...,\zeta_{m}(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}(\bm{s}),...,\zeta_{m}(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math> be defined by <span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</span>. A function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a High Order Control Barrier Function (HOCBF) of relative degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for system (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) if there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m-i)^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m-i)^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> order differentiable class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> functions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i},\ i\in\{1,...,m-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i},\ i\in\{1,...,m-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}[L_{f}^{m}b(\bm{s})+L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b(\bm{s})\bm{a}+\Omega(b(\bm{s}))+\alpha_{m}(\zeta_{m-1}(\bm{s}))]\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}[L_{f}^{m}b(\bm{s})+L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b(\bm{s})\bm{a}+\Omega(b(\bm{s}))+\alpha_{m}(\zeta_{m-1}(\bm{s}))]\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}\in\bigcap_{i=1}^{m}C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}\in\bigcap_{i=1}^{m}C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Definition 3.6 (High Order CBF (HOCBF) ( ) ). ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes derivative along <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> times and one time respectively, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(.)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the remaining Lie derivative along <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with degree less than or equal to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> (omitted for simplicity, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the HOCBF in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Definition 3.6 (High Order CBF (HOCBF) ( ) ). ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is a general form of the degree one CBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and exponential CBF in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. The following theorem on HOCBFs implies the forward invariance property of the CBFs and the original safety set. The proof is omitted (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> for the proof).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Control Lyapunov function (CLF)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A continuously differentiable function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a globally and exponentially stabilizing CLF for (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) if there exists constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}||\bm{s}||^{2}\leq V(\bm{s})\leq c_{2}||\bm{s}||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}||\bm{s}||^{2}\leq V(\bm{s})\leq c_{2}||\bm{s}||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the following inequality holds</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}[L_{f}V(\bm{s})+L_{g}V(\bm{s})a+\eta(\bm{s})]\leq e," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{U}}[L_{f}V(\bm{s})+L_{g}V(\bm{s})a+\eta(\bm{s})]\leq e,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> makes this a soft constraint.
If the dynamics are of the form: <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\dot{s}}=f(\bm{s},\bm{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\dot{s}}=f(\bm{s},\bm{a})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can express it in the form:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\dot{s}}^{\prime}=f^{\prime}(\bm{s}^{\prime},\bm{a})+g^{\prime}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\bm{a}^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\dot{s}}^{\prime}=f^{\prime}(\bm{s}^{\prime},\bm{a})+g^{\prime}(\bm{s}^{\prime})\bm{a}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{s}\\
\bm{a}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝒔</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝒂</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{s}\\
\bm{a}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the augmented system states and new primitive actions of the system respectively, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}f(\bm{s},\bm{a})\\
\bm{0}_{\text{dim}(\bm{a})}\end{bmatrix},\text{and }g^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{0}_{\text{dim}(\bm{s})\times\text{dim}(\bm{a^{\prime}})}\\
\bm{B}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>dim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝑩</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}f(\bm{s},\bm{a})\\
\bm{0}_{\text{dim}(\bm{a})}\end{bmatrix},\text{and }g^{\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{0}_{\text{dim}(\bm{s})\times\text{dim}(\bm{a^{\prime}})}\\
\bm{B}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\bm{a})\times\text{dim}(\bm{a}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>dim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\bm{a})\times\text{dim}(\bm{a}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Generally, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains ones in its diagonal entries. The redefined dynamics can be used subsequently for defining CBFs.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.14850v1/HMARL-CBF-Final.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/230;" width="381" height="230" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
At the higher level, agents choose skills in a decentralized manner based on <math id="S4.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on their own observation, whereas at the lower level every agent <math id="S4.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> executes these skills by learning a parametric CBF-based policy <math id="S4.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pi_{z^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pi_{z^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on their observation and the skill. The extrinsic trajectory return <math id="S4.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{H}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{H}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is used to jointly learn the cooperative policy for all the agents centrally, and the intrinsic trajectory return <math id="S4.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{L}^{i}(\tau^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{L}^{i}(\tau^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for agent <math id="S4.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s trajectory <math id="S4.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to learn the policies corresponding to the skills. The high level and low level policy networks are shared among all agents.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a cooperative multi-agent setting among a set of agents. Let the set of agents be denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=\{1,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=\{1,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider that every agent <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> has dynamics as in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We partition the state space into two disjoint spaces corresponding to the agent states and the environment states. The environment state is denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{e}\in\mathcal{S}^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{e}\in\mathcal{S}^{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. The state of an agent <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{i}\in\mathcal{S}^{i}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{i}\in\mathcal{S}^{i}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the control input vector is denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{i}\in\mathcal{A}^{i}=[\bm{a}_{min},\bm{a}_{max}]\subset\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{i}\in\mathcal{A}^{i}=[\bm{a}_{min},\bm{a}_{max}]\subset\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the control input constraint set for the agent <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{min},\bm{a}_{\max}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{min},\bm{a}_{\max}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the joint state space is denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{S}^{i}\times\mathcal{S}^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{S}^{i}\times\mathcal{S}^{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the joint action space of the agents is denoted by <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{A}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{A}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our setting, there is a performance related cost function <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s},\bm{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s},\bm{a})</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the system specification, where <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (more specifically <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{S}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{S}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without loss of generality, in our cooperative setting, the stage cost is defined in the form <math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s},\bm{a})=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}}l^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s},\bm{a})=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}}l^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stage cost associated with agent <math id="S4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, associated with every agent <math id="S4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of safety-related functions <math id="S4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b^{i}_{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})\}_{i\in\mathcal{N}}^{j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b^{i}_{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})\}_{i\in\mathcal{N}}^{j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{j}:\mathcal{S}^{i}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{j}:\mathcal{S}^{i}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of states of other agents or the environment which is dependent on the global state <math id="S4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume the constraints are with respect to agents or area of the environment within a neighborhood
of an agent observable to it. We consider a partially observable setting whereby <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}^{i}\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}^{i}\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observation space of agent <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,\mathcal{S}^{i}\subseteq\mathcal{O}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,\mathcal{S}^{i}\subseteq\mathcal{O}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the joint observation space <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{O}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{O}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the constraints can be alternatively expressed as <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{i}_{j}(\bm{o}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{i}_{j}(\bm{o}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{o}^{i}\in\mathcal{O}^{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{o}^{i}\in\mathcal{O}^{i},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{i}\in\mathcal{O}^{i},s^{j}\in\mathcal{O}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{i}\in\mathcal{O}^{i},s^{j}\in\mathcal{O}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the set of constraints as <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the constraints are initially satisfied upon their introduction. hus, our safety-constrained problem is expressed as an Stochastic Optimal Control Problem (SOCP):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\pi\in\Pi}\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}l(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\right]\quad\text{s.t.}\quad b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{s}_{t}^{j})\geq 0,\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{N},\;j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}}),\;\forall t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}l(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\right]\quad\text{s.t.}\quad b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{s}_{t}^{j})\geq 0,\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{N},\;j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}}),\;\forall t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(.|\bm{s})\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(.|\bm{s})\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint state feedback policy of the agents i.e. <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of distributions over the support of the random variable.
It is noteworthy that this problem requires that the constraints are satisfied pointwise in time for every trajectory generated by <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cooperative setting with pointwise-in-time hard constraints vs. CMDP setting:</span>
As shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, CMDPs address safety in fully cooperative multi-agent settings where agents share a common reward and seek to minimize the expected cumulative cost:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{\pi}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}l\left(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}\right)\right]\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{\pi}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}b_{j}^{i}\left(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\right)\right]\leq\alpha_{j}^{i},;\forall j" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{\pi}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}l\left(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}\right)\right]\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{\pi}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}b_{j}^{i}\left(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\right)\right]\leq\alpha_{j}^{i},;\forall j</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a state-feedback policy, and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a trajectory generated by <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. While structurally similar to pointwise-in-time constraints in the single-agent setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, the multi-agent CMDP formulation requires policies to depend on the joint state <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, making decentralized execution infeasible in partially observable environments and linearly increasing constraint complexity with the number of agents. Our formulation overcomes these limitations by enabling decentralized safety guarantees in partially observable, safety-critical multi-agent systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF)</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel Safe Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (HMARL) approach illustrated in figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to tackle the aforementioned problem in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Specifically, we decompose the problem in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) into a bi-level RL problem. The higher-level problem objective concerns the optimization of the joint performance of the agents.
The lower-level optimization problem addresses the safety of the agents defined using the second term in the stage cost in (<a href="#S5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 5.1 Multi-Agent High-Level Policy Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Our proposed HMARL method is based on the idea of skills which is defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safe agent skills.</span> Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, we define a safety-constrained skill <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for an agent <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as a seven-tuple: <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i},\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i},s^{e}_{init}),T_{max},\Phi_{z}^{i},\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}),r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}),\pi_{z}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i},\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i},s^{e}_{init}),T_{max},\Phi_{z}^{i},\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}),r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}),\pi_{z}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i}\subset\mathcal{S}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i}\subset\mathcal{S}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the initiation set for the skill respectively, <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i}\in\mathcal{I}_{z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{e}_{init}\in\mathcal{S}^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{e}_{init}\in\mathcal{S}^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> are the observation of agent <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and environment state at the time of initiation of the skill, <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{init}^{i},\bm{s}^{e}_{init})\subseteq\mathcal{O}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{init}^{i},\bm{s}^{e}_{init})\subseteq\mathcal{O}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the termination set of the skill, and <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{z}^{i}:\mathcal{\mathcal{O}}^{i}\times\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{z}^{i}:\mathcal{\mathcal{O}}^{i}\times\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the termination function as defined below:</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{z}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i},t)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\bm{o}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i},\bm{s}^{e}_{init})\text{ or, }t\geq T_{\max}\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or, </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{z}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i},t)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\bm{o}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}(\bm{o}_{z,init}^{i},\bm{s}^{e}_{init})\text{ or, }t\geq T_{\max}\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">An skill is terminated as a function of the initiation state of the skill <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in one of the two ways: i. either upon observing that the skill has been executed or, ii. interruption due to a timeout for exceeding the maximum allowed duration of execution, <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\textrm{max}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\textrm{max}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{i}(\bm{o}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of safety constraints for agent <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> that has to be satisfied during the execution of the skill. We define a reward function <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to action <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which is used to learn the skill. Finally, <math id="S5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{z}^{i}:\mathcal{O}^{i}\times\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{z}^{i}:\mathcal{O}^{i}\times\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the safe skill policy expressed as a SOCP as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi^{i}_{z},k}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi^{i}_{z},k}[\sum_{t=0}^{k}r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|\bm{s}_{0}^{i}=\bm{s}^{i}_{init})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi^{i}_{z},k}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi^{i}_{z},k}[\sum_{t=0}^{k}r_{z}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|\bm{s}_{0}^{i}=\bm{s}^{i}_{init})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to }b_{i}^{j}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t})\geq 0,\ \bm{s}_{k}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z},\ k\leq T_{max},\ \forall b_{i}^{j}\in\mathcal{N}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}),\quad\forall t=0,\dots,k;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>subject to </mtext><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to }b_{i}^{j}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t})\geq 0,\ \bm{s}_{k}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z},\ k\leq T_{max},\ \forall b_{i}^{j}\in\mathcal{N}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}),\quad\forall t=0,\dots,k;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The set of skills for an agent <math id="S5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the joint set of skills of the <math id="S5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents is defined as <math id="S5.p2.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{Z}=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{Z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{Z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define a high-level policy for the <math id="S5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents over their set of skills denoted by <math id="S5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}:\mathcal{O}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}:\mathcal{O}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}\in\Pi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}\in\Pi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, which becomes the high-level RL problem. Subsequently, we define <math id="S5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{z}}:\mathcal{O}\times\mathcal{J}_{\bm{z}}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{z}}:\mathcal{O}\times\mathcal{J}_{\bm{z}}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> as the joint policy for the joint skill <math id="S5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S5.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> agents, where <math id="S5.p2.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\bm{z}}=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{J}_{z}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\bm{z}}=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{J}_{z}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Learning the safe skills of the agents becomes our low-level RL problem. Next, we detail our proposed HMARL-CBF method.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Multi-Agent High-Level Policy Learning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, the high-level policy is formulated as a joint centralized optimization problem involving all the agents over their set of skills. The optimization problem can be solved using existing CTDE schemes, either using policy gradients or value decomposition. The higher level problem can be modeled as an MSMDP with the corresponding extrinsic reward for state <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and skill <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> executed in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> steps using policy <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as: <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\bm{z}}}[\sum_{t=0}^{k-1}\gamma^{t}r_{H}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}|\bm{s}_{0}=\bm{s})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\bm{s},\bm{z},k)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\bm{z}}}[\sum_{t=0}^{k-1}\gamma^{t}r_{H}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}|\bm{s}_{0}=\bm{s})]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call this extrinsic as it is task-specific and, hence, chosen to be the environment reward. This is revised by incorporating safety corresponding to (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) rendering the following extrinsic reward:</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{H}(\bm{s},\bm{a})=-\bigg[l(\bm{s},\bm{a})-\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})}p_{i}^{j}\mathbb{I}(b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j}))b(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})\bigg]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{H}(\bm{s},\bm{a})=-\bigg[l(\bm{s},\bm{a})-\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})}p_{i}^{j}\mathbb{I}(b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j}))b(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}\in\mathcal{S},\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}\in\mathcal{S},\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function defined as follows.</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(x)=\begin{cases}1,\text{ if }x&lt;0\text{ for some }i,\\
0,\text{ otherwise. }\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> otherwise. </mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(x)=\begin{cases}1,\text{ if }x&lt;0\text{ for some }i,\\
0,\text{ otherwise. }\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The minimization problem is thus mapped to a maximization problem of accumulated reward. Then, the problem in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) can be mapped to the following problem:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}[R_{H}(\tau)]=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{\infty}\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}}R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}}|\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\sim\pi_{H})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}[R_{H}(\tau)]=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{\infty}\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}}R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}}|\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\sim\pi_{H})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}},k_{t^{\prime}}}\bigg[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}r_{H}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\bigg]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}},k_{t^{\prime}}}\bigg[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}r_{H}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\bigg]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\pi_{H}\in\Pi_{H}}J(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\pi_{H}\in\Pi_{H}}J(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of the high-level policies and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a trajectory sampled from the joint composition of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The policy <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> given a joint skills policy of the agents <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> optimizes the trajectories with respect to the reward in (<a href="#S5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 5.1 Multi-Agent High-Level Policy Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). This problem can be solved using any existing on-policy or off-policy MARL algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. If we parameterize the policy with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy gradient of the parametrized policy <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as derived in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> is given by:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}J(\pi_{H_{\theta}},\pi_{\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}J(\pi_{H_{\theta}},\pi_{\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H_{\theta}}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{H_{\theta}}(\bm{z}_{t}|\bm{s}_{t})\right.\left.\bigg(\sum_{t^{\prime}=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}})\bigg)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim{(\pi_{H_{\theta}}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{H_{\theta}}(\bm{z}_{t}|\bm{s}_{t})\right.\left.\bigg(\sum_{t^{\prime}=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}})\bigg)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under a CTDE scheme, in order to execute the policies de-centrally, the agent policies/actor network (parameters) can be learned individually agent-wise conditioned on their observation (and history <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) i.e., <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}^{i}(\text{ }|\bm{o}^{i}_{t},h^{i}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}^{i}(\text{ }|\bm{o}^{i}_{t},h^{i}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, using a centralized critic network. Alternatively, the policy <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be learnt by minimizing the following (Q-learning) loss using
a parametrization <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}+1},k_{t^{\prime}}}[(R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}})+\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}}\max_{\bm{z}\in\Pi_{\bm{z}}}Q^{tot}_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}+1},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}+1})-Q^{tot}_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}))^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}+1},k_{t^{\prime}}}[(R(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}},k_{t^{\prime}})+\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}}\max_{\bm{z}\in\Pi_{\bm{z}}}Q^{tot}_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}+1},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}+1})-Q^{tot}_{\theta}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}))^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{tot}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{tot}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total state-action value as defined in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Semi-Markov Decision Process ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) which can be learned using value decomposition based on Individual Global Max principle proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The low-level policy is associated with execution of the skills selected by the agents based on the high-level policy. We define an intrinsic step reward conditioned on any skill <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of any agent <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}|z^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}|z^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to incorporate intrinsic motivation. Notice, the safety constraints are included in the extrinsic step reward <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, to ensure safe execution of the skill, we formulate the low-level policy as a Constrained Optimal Control Problem (COCP) as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}\in\Pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},k_{t^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}\in\Pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},k_{t^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},\bm{s}_{t^{\prime},\dots,k_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},k_{t^{\prime}}}\left[\sum_{t=t^{\prime}}^{k_{t^{\prime}}}r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|z^{i}_{t^{\prime}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},\bm{s}_{t^{\prime},\dots,k_{t^{\prime}}}^{i},k_{t^{\prime}}}\left[\sum_{t=t^{\prime}}^{k_{t^{\prime}}}r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|z^{i}_{t^{\prime}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S5.Ex6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">subject to</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})\geq 0\ \ \forall\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t},j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}}),t\in[t^{\prime},\dots k_{t^{\prime}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})\geq 0\ \ \forall\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t},j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}}),t\in[t^{\prime},\dots k_{t^{\prime}}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}}^{i}\in\mathcal{S}^{i},\bm{s}_{k_{t^{\prime}}}\in\mathcal{J}_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}}^{i}\in\mathcal{S}^{i},\bm{s}_{k_{t^{\prime}}}\in\mathcal{J}_{z_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the time the skill was initiated by agent <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to solve the problem we formulate a <em id="S5.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameterized</em> constrained optimization problem based on CBFs at each time step <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that gives us the deterministic policy as a function of the observation conditioned on the skill <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i};\bm{\phi}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i};\bm{\phi}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. We include control Lyapunov functions (CLFs) <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as functions of the state <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the terminal state <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{k_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z_{t^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{k_{t^{\prime}}}^{i}\in\mathcal{J}^{i}_{z_{t^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to assist in learning the optimal trajectory corresponding to the skill and terminate by converging to the termination set with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>.This is similar to stabilizing the system at the terminal state. Note that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{k_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{k_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be a terminal state, but any state-related Lyapunov function that aids in learning the skill. This results in a convex optimization problem, more specifically a QP that requires low compute. The optimization problem corresponding to the deterministic policy <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i};\bm{\phi}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{i}(\bm{o}^{i}_{t}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i};\bm{\phi}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is given below.</p>
<table id="S5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\in\mathcal{A}^{i},\bm{e}}\quad\bm{a}_{t}^{i\top}H(\bm{\phi}_{H}^{i})\bm{a}_{t}^{i}+F^{\top}(\bm{\phi}_{F}^{i})\bm{a}_{t}^{i}+\bm{\phi}_{e}^{\top}\bm{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒆</mi></mrow></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\in\mathcal{A}^{i},\bm{e}}\quad\bm{a}_{t}^{i\top}H(\bm{\phi}_{H}^{i})\bm{a}_{t}^{i}+F^{\top}(\bm{\phi}_{F}^{i})\bm{a}_{t}^{i}+\bm{\phi}_{e}^{\top}\bm{e}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S5.Ex7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">subject to</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{f}^{m}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})+L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})\bm{a}^{i}_{t}\Omega(b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b})+\alpha_{m}(\zeta_{m-1}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b})\geq 0;\ \forall j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{f}^{m}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})+L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t})\bm{a}^{i}_{t}\Omega(b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b})+\alpha_{m}(\zeta_{m-1}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}^{j}_{t});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b})\geq 0;\ \forall j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{f}V_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i})+L_{g}V^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i})\bm{a}^{i}_{t}+\eta(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i};\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,j})\leq e^{i,j};\ \forall j=1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">;</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{f}V_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i})+L_{g}V^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i})\bm{a}^{i}_{t}+\eta(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{s}_{k_{t}}^{i};\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,j})\leq e^{i,j};\ \forall j=1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}^{i}=[\bm{\phi}_{H}^{i},\bm{\phi}_{F}^{i},\bm{\phi}_{b}^{i,1},\dots,\bm{\phi}_{b}^{i,|\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}})|},\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,1},\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,2},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}^{i}=[\bm{\phi}_{H}^{i},\bm{\phi}_{F}^{i},\bm{\phi}_{b}^{i,1},\dots,\bm{\phi}_{b}^{i,|\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}_{t}})|},\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,1},\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,2},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math> are the parameters of the optimization problem such that each term in the vector <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})\times\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>dim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})\times\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{R}^{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the slack term from the CLF constraints. The chosen quadratic objective, combined with the affine CBF constraints, forms a Quadratic Program (QP) at each time step <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which is well suited for real-time safety-critical control applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our case, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen to be positive definite as the objective generally includes tracking error/control effort minimization, which makes the problem strictly convex. The parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned as a function of the agent <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s observation <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{o}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{o}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and parameterized by <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}^{i}=\Gamma_{\bm{\nu}^{i}}(\bm{o}^{i}|z^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msup><mi>𝝂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}^{i}=\Gamma_{\bm{\nu}^{i}}(\bm{o}^{i}|z^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable w.r.t to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The function can be a deterministic or stochastic function of a continuous random variable <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> using the reparameterization trick in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. With the reparameterization trick, the control policy becomes stochastic, thus allowing for use of stochastic policy gradient algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An agent’s trajectory <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{i}=\{\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0},\bm{s}^{i}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1},\bm{s}^{i}_{2},\bm{a}^{i}_{2},\dots\}=\{\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{z}^{i}_{0},\bm{s}^{i}_{k_{0}},\bm{z}^{i}_{k_{0}},\bm{s}^{i}_{k_{1}},\bm{z}^{i}_{k_{1}},\dots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{i}=\{\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0},\bm{s}^{i}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1},\bm{s}^{i}_{2},\bm{a}^{i}_{2},\dots\}=\{\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{z}^{i}_{0},\bm{s}^{i}_{k_{0}},\bm{z}^{i}_{k_{0}},\bm{s}^{i}_{k_{1}},\bm{z}^{i}_{k_{1}},\dots\}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as a sequence of states and actions indexed by time as well as states and skills indexed by time on a different scale. Additionally, the forward invariant property of CBFs can guarantee that the trajectory resides in the safe set. In order to jointly learn with the high-level policy from the sampled trajectories, we map the safe skill learning problem for any agent <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.Ex5" title="In 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) as a trajectory optimization problem with the policy <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{i}_{z_{\bm{\nu}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>𝝂</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{i}_{z_{\bm{\nu}}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the following objective:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}}^{-i},\pi^{i}_{z_{\bm{\nu}}})=\mathbb{E}_{\tau^{i}\sim(\pi_{H}\circ(\pi_{\bm{z}}^{-i},\pi_{z_{\bm{\nu}}}^{i}))}[R_{L}^{i}(\tau^{i})]=\ \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|z^{i}_{t}\sim\pi_{H}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}\sim\pi^{i}_{{z_{t}}_{\bm{\nu}}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>𝝂</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>𝝂</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>𝝂</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}}^{-i},\pi^{i}_{z_{\bm{\nu}}})=\mathbb{E}_{\tau^{i}\sim(\pi_{H}\circ(\pi_{\bm{z}}^{-i},\pi_{z_{\bm{\nu}}}^{i}))}[R_{L}^{i}(\tau^{i})]=\ \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{t},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}|z^{i}_{t}\sim\pi_{H}^{i},\bm{a}^{i}_{t}\sim\pi^{i}_{{z_{t}}_{\bm{\nu}}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We drop the policy parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and superscript <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid overloading the symbols.
Here, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{z}^{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{z}^{-i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint skills policy of the other agents except agent <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. we solve the optimization problem in (<a href="#S5.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) by learning the parameters of the QP in (<a href="#S5.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) that maximizes the total reward. The problem in (<a href="#S5.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) can be solved using policy gradient (PG) methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The satisfaction of the CBF constraints <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) guarantees safe execution of the skills, thus guaranteeing the safety of our proposed HMARL approach.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given, the constraints <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are satisfied at the initial time, the satisfaction of the CBF constraints makes the corresponding safety sets forward invariant per Theorem (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 ( ( ) ). ‣ 3.2 Control Barrier Functions ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), thus guaranteeing their satisfaction at all future times. This makes the policies corresponding to the skills of the agents safe, thus, guaranteeing the safety of our proposed HMARL approach.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that robust CBFs can be used in the presence of model uncertainty following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> to achieve similar safety guarantees. Besides that, we detail how the high-level task specific reward can be incorporated to the low-level skill learning to align the low level learning to the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> by applying the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker condition on the Lagrangian to derive the gradient of the state feedback policy with respect to its parameters.
Specifically, let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> denote the dual variables on the HOCBF and CLF constraints, let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> create a diagonal matrix from a vector, and let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}^{*},\lambda^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}^{*},\lambda^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the optimal solutions of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We can then write <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\bm{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝝁</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\bm{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> in the form:</p>
<table id="S5.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\begin{array}[]{c}d_{\mu}\\
d_{\lambda}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}H&amp;G^{T}D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)\\
G&amp;D\left(G\bm{a}^{*}-h\right)\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left[\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{1}_{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}\\
0\end{array}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>H</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>G</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mtext>dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\begin{array}[]{c}d_{\mu}\\
d_{\lambda}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}H&amp;G^{T}D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)\\
G&amp;D\left(G\bm{a}^{*}-h\right)\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left[\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{1}_{\text{dim}(\mathcal{A}_{i})}\\
0\end{array}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G,h</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated by <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{j}_{b},G^{j}_{v},h^{j}_{b},h^{j}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{j}_{b},G^{j}_{v},h^{j}_{b},h^{j}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G^{j}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G^{j}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j});\ \ j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-L_{g}L_{f}^{m-1}b^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j});\ \ j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G^{j}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G^{j}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-L_{g}V^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,j});\ \ j=1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-L_{g}V^{j}_{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}_{v}^{i,j});\ \ j=1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{b}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{b}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{f}^{m}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})+\Omega\left(b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b}\right)+\alpha_{m}\left(\zeta_{m-1}\left(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b}\right)\right);j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{f}^{m}b_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})+\Omega\left(b_{j}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j});\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b}\right)+\alpha_{m}\left(\zeta_{m-1}\left(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{b}\right)\right);j\in\mathcal{N}^{i}({\bm{s}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{b}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{b}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{f}V_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})+\eta(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{v});\ \ j=1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{f}V_{i}^{j}(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j})+\eta(\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{j};\bm{\phi}^{i,j}_{v});\ \ j=1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">As the control bounds in (<a href="#S5.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) are not trainable, they are not included in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the relevant gradients with respect to all the parameters are given by</p>
<table id="S5.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{H}\bm{\mu}=\frac{1}{2}\left(d_{\bm{u}}\bm{u}^{T}+\bm{u}d_{\bm{u}}^{T}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{H}\bm{\mu}=\frac{1}{2}\left(d_{\bm{u}}\bm{u}^{T}+\bm{u}d_{\bm{u}}^{T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{F}\bm{\mu}=d_{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>F</mi></msub><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{F}\bm{\mu}=d_{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{G}\bm{\mu}=D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)\left(d_{\lambda}\bm{u}^{T}+\lambda d_{\bm{u}}^{T}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{G}\bm{\mu}=D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)\left(d_{\lambda}\bm{u}^{T}+\lambda d_{\bm{u}}^{T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{h}\bm{\mu}=-D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)d_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{h}\bm{\mu}=-D\left(\lambda^{*}\right)d_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">With the hierarchical composition of the policies <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the augmented state <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\hat{s}}=[\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{-i},\bm{z},\bm{s}_{\bm{z},init}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\hat{s}}=[\bm{s}^{i},\bm{s}^{-i},\bm{z},\bm{s}_{\bm{z},init}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{-i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state of the agents other than agent <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{\bm{z},init}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{\bm{z},init}</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint state of the agents at the time of the initiation of the joint skill <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The initiation state is included as the joint termination of the skill depends on the initiation state. The state and action value functions for agent <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to reward <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{L}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{L}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, skill policy <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{z^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{z^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> (given the high level policy <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and the skill policy other agents <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}}</annotation></semantics></math>) are defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{\hat{s}}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0}|\bm{\hat{s}}_{0}=\bm{\hat{s}})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}_{1}}[Q(\bm{\hat{s}}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1})]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{\hat{s}}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0}|\bm{\hat{s}}_{0}=\bm{\hat{s}})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}_{1}}[Q(\bm{\hat{s}}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1})]]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0}|\bm{s}^{i}_{0}=\bm{s}^{i},z^{i})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}_{1}}[Q(\bm{\hat{s}}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1})]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{s}^{i}_{0},\bm{a}^{i}_{0}|\bm{s}^{i}_{0}=\bm{s}^{i},z^{i})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}_{1}}[Q(\bm{\hat{s}}_{1},\bm{a}^{i}_{1})]]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The state-action value function for agent <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, given the hierarchical policy composition <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H}\circ\pi_{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})=\mathbb{E}\left[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}^{\prime}}\left[V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}}^{\prime})\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})=\mathbb{E}\left[r_{L}^{i}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\hat{s}}^{\prime}}\left[V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}}^{\prime})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The corresponding advantage function is given by:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})=Q^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})-V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})=Q^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}},\bm{a}^{i})-V^{\pi_{z^{i}}}(\bm{\hat{s}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Based on the above definition of value functions and advantage function, as well the gradient of QP controller output with respect to its parameters, standard on-policy and off-policy algorithms can be used to derive the policy gradient to learn the controller parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-level task specific reward to low level learning.</span> The low-level policies should be such that they optimize the entire trajectory given by the problem in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 Problem Formulation ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Hence, in addition to introducing intrinsic motivation through the low-level reward, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, we introduce an advantage term to the low-level reward resulting in the following revised low-level reward:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})=\lambda\frac{A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})}{\mathcal{N}}+(1-\lambda)r^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})=\lambda\frac{A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})}{\mathcal{N}}+(1-\lambda)r^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\leq t</annotation></semantics></math> is the time when the joint skill at time <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> was initiated. The high-level advantage <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{a}_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{a}_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as below:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})=Q^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})-V^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})=Q^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})-V^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}}\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}}\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{H}}(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> are as defined in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Semi-Markov Decision Process ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Semi-Markov Decision Process ‣ 3 Background ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) respectively. The parameter <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to balance between skill learning and optimization of the joint behavior of the agents. From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> we have thatfor high level policies the following holds:</p>
<table id="S5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\tilde{\pi}_{H})=J(\pi_{H})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\sim\pi_{H}}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\tilde{\pi}_{H})=J(\pi_{H})+\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\sim\pi_{H}}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the low level reward in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="Equation 28 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>), the revised low level learning objective <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for agent <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})\approx\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}(1-\gamma)}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}{\gamma^{t^{\prime}}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]+(1-\lambda)J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})\approx\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}(1-\gamma)}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}{\gamma^{t^{\prime}}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]+(1-\lambda)J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Based on <a href="#S5.E28" title="Equation 28 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>, we can write our low level policy’s learning objective for agent <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as the following:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E32X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E32X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{J}^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{J}^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E32Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E32Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}\left(\lambda\frac{A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)}{\mathcal{N}}+(1-\lambda)\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right)\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}\left(\lambda\frac{A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)}{\mathcal{N}}+(1-\lambda)\tilde{r}^{i}_{L}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right)\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E32Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E32Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\frac{\gamma^{t^{\prime}}(1-\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}})}{1-\gamma}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\frac{\gamma^{t^{\prime}}(1-\gamma^{k_{t^{\prime}}})}{1-\gamma}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E32Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E32Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+(1-\lambda)\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}r^{i}_{L_{A}}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+(1-\lambda)\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}\sim\pi_{z^{i}_{t^{\prime}}}}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{k_{t^{\prime}}-1}\gamma^{t+k_{t^{\prime}}}r^{i}_{L_{A}}(\bm{s}_{t}^{i},\bm{a}_{t}^{i}|z_{t^{\prime}}^{i})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E32Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E32Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}(1-\gamma)}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]+(1-\lambda)J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{\lambda}{\mathcal{N}(1-\gamma)}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}t^{\prime}=0\end{subarray}}\gamma^{t^{\prime}}A^{\pi_{H}}\left(\bm{s}_{t^{\prime}},\bm{z}_{t^{\prime}}\right)\right]+(1-\lambda)J^{i}(\pi_{H},\pi_{\bm{z}^{-i}},\pi_{z^{i}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, the low level reward for every agent can be revised as above to align skill learning process with the task learning process, as the first term is related to the high level policy return.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Environment Details</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our proposed method on traffic scenarios involving complex road networks with multiple agents that must ensure safety concerning neighboring agents while navigating toward their destinations. We use the MetaDrive simulator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> to implement, validate, and compare our method against existing baselines in a cooperative setting, where agents (autonomous vehicles) collaborate to improve the overall traffic efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The simulator includes five environments for multi-agent experiments. We implement our algorithm in four of the originally proposed environments and design a new environment to simulate an on-ramp merging roadway, replacing the parking lot environment. Since safety is less challenging in parking lots due to low vehicle speeds, the problem can be formulated and solved as a static optimization problem with a fixed number of vehicles and parking spaces. The five environments from the METADRIVE simulator are illustrated in the figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.1 Environment Details ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. As mentioned these environments involve regions where agents can cross path with other agents, Hence, it is necessary for the agents to cooperate but at the safe time stay safe with each other to achieve the task i.e. reach destination successfully and timely. In all five environments used in our experiments, the vehicles are initialized at random spawn points, and assigned destinations to each of them randomly. The agents are terminated in three situations: reaching the destinations (deemed as successful), crashing with others or driving out of the road (deemed as failure). Once an existing vehicle is terminated new vehicles are spawned immediately to ensure continuous traffic flow. The terminated vehicles remains static for 10 steps (2s) in the original positions, creating impermeable obstacles. If a vehicle crashes into other terminated vehicles before it is removed from the environment, it is also considered as failure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.14850v1/envs1.png" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of the various METADRIVE environments.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we evaluate our method on a suite of lidar-based environments introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.1 Environment Details ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. All environments are partially observable, with agents equipped with lidar sensors. In the <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Target</span> environments, each agent is assigned a fixed goal and must reach it while avoiding collisions with other agents and obstacles. The primary distinction between the <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Target</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Target-Bicycle</span> environments lies in the underlying agent dynamics; further details are provided in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.1 Environment Details ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In the <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spread</span> environment, agents must cooperate to cover all goal locations while avoiding both obstacles and one another.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.14850v1/Additional_envs.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/139;" width="381" height="139" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Illustration of the lidar based environments which include: (a) target environment, (b) spread environment and (c) target bicycle environment.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Experimental Details and Additional Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin this section by presenting the details of the training, evaluation and ablation experiments. Besides that, we include results for the generalization experiments used to validate our proposed method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have implemented our proposed method using both on-policy and off-policy algorithms. The on-policy implementation was done using MAPPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> with PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> for the skills. The off-policy implementation used QMIX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> for the higher level policy and SAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> for learning the agent skills.
The extrinsic reward was chosen to be same as that defined in MetaDrive (which includes the energy, time, driving reward, lane deviation, collision penalty) of all the AVs in the environment. Besides that we include some regularization terms corresponding to our skills to avoid overfitting to any specific skill(s). The algorithms were implemented using RLLib; an open-source framework for ML. Further details about the implementation can be found in <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 Experimental Details and Additional Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.1 </span>Algorithm details</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method can be implemented using both existing on-policy and off-policy RL algorithms. The bilevel RL problems presented in (<a href="#S5.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 5.1 Multi-Agent High-Level Policy Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and (<a href="#S5.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ 5.2 CBF-Based Individual Safe Skill Learning ‣ 5 Safe Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HMARL-CBF) ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), respectively, should be solved sequentially. However, as previous works illustrated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, jointly optimizing both policies by updating their corresponding parameters alternatively renders similar performances, which is consistent with our observation from the experiments. Based on this observation, the algorithm is presented in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 6.3.1 Algorithm details ‣ 6.3 Implementation Details ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ HMARL-CBF – Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Control Barrier Functions for Safety-Critical Autonomous Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The high-level policy <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposed into agent-level parameterized policies <math id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{i}_{H_{\bm{\theta}^{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{i}_{H_{\bm{\theta}^{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn decentralized policies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with CBFs</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
               Initialize randomly the high-level policy parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> of the low-level policy parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">//network parameter sharing done between agents.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
               Set <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Grouping" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Grouping</annotation></semantics></math> flag.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each episode <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}^{i}\sim\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}(z^{i}|\bm{o}_{0}^{i}),\ \forall i\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒐</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}^{i}\sim\pi_{H_{\bm{\theta}}}(z^{i}|\bm{o}_{0}^{i}),\ \forall i\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">// sample initial skill
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                Set data buffer <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
